This isn't necessarily the case -- if you want a specific header
tacked onto all outbound electronic mail, you'll probably want to
tweak the MTA (sendmail, smail, whatever) to do this.  Using Unix
smail 3.1.28, for example, I might add this to my lib/transports file:

append_header="${if and{{origin:local}{!header:organization}} \
Organization: Yoyodyne Propulsion Systems -- standard disclaimers apply}",

It's not a given that everyone will have a .signature file unless you
give one to them, and it could very well be the case that someone
would want to change the .signature file and potentially eliminate the
disclaimer there.  Tweaking headers around typically takes a little
bit more awareness -- if someone replaces the default X-Disclaimer:
header with something else, they probably know what they're doing.

